T*(arguments to end this litigation. It used)Tj
T*(to be extremely difficult to modify any)Tj
T*(kind of equitable decree. See United)Tj
T*(States v. Swift & Co., 286 U.S. 106)Tj
T*(\(1932\). But as we noted recently in)Tj
T*(ordering radical modification of another)Tj
T*(institutional reform decree, one that had)Tj
T*(subjected the Chicago police to severe)Tj
T*(restrictions on its power to investigate)Tj
T*(terrorist activities, the Supreme Court)Tj
T*(has adopted a much more flexible standard)Tj
T*(for the modification of decrees entered)Tj
T*(in institutional reform litigation than)Tj
T*(the Swift standard of yore. Alliance to)Tj
T*(End Repression v. City of Chicago, 237)Tj
T*(F.3d 799, 800-01 \(7th Cir. 2001\); see)Tj
T*(Rufo v. Inmates of Suffolk County Jail,)Tj
T*(502 U.S. 367, 378-81 \(1992\); Board of)Tj
T*(Education v. Dowell, supra, 498 U.S. at)Tj
T*(248; see also Alexander v. Britt, 89 F.3d)Tj
T*(194, 197-98 \(4th Cir. 1996\). The Court)Tj
T*(believes the states and their)Tj
T*(subdivisions have a right to the)Tj
T*(restoration of control over the)Tj
T*(institutions of state and local)Tj
T*(government as soon as the objectives of)Tj
T*(the federal remedial decree have been)Tj
T*(achieved. Missouri v. Jenkins, supra, 515)Tj
T*(U.S. at 99; Bogard v. Wright, 159 F.3d)Tj
T*(1060, 1065 \(7th Cir. 1998\); People Who)Tj
T*(Care v. Rockford Board of Education, 153)Tj
T*(F.3d 834 \(7th Cir. 1998\) \(per curiam\);)Tj
T*(United States v. Board of School)Tj
T*(Commissioners, supra, 128 F.3d at 510.)Tj
T*(Unlike decrees that bind private parties,)Tj
T*(decrees that hand over the control of)Tj
T*(important state functions, such as)Tj
T*(education, to federal courts "are not)Tj
T*(intended to operate in perpetuity." Board)Tj
T*(of Education v. Dowell, supra, 498 U.S.)Tj
T*(at 248. "[T]he Supreme Court disfavors)Tj
T*(permanent injunctions in school cases.)Tj
T*(The administration of public schools is a)Tj
T*(state executive function rather than a)Tj
T*(federal judicial function, and so ought)Tj
T*(not to be subjected to the perpetual)Tj
T*(tutelage of the federal courts." United)Tj
T*(States v. Board of School Commissioners,)Tj
T*(supra, 128 F.3d at 510.)Tj
T*()Tj
T*(  The purpose of a school desegration)Tj
T*(decree is to eliminate the consequences)Tj
T*(of segregation. When they have been)Tj
T*(eliminated the decree has done its job)Tj
T*(and should be lifted. This simple)Tj
T*(principle, which we don't understand the)Tj
T*(plaintiffs to be quarreling with,)Tj
T*(dictates our decision. The Rockford)Tj
T*(public schools have been desegregated. No)Tj
T*(longer are there any schools that are)Tj
ET

